#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068726 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068476} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6c5d24fa-8a65-5c20-bb50-530ca8dcf061" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050315}] :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069138} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069173} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069341} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487300} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487346} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492907} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492916} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545849} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547069} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186659687} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186659702} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186659717} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186659735} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186659739}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving Equations With Fractional Solutions"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068727 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068477} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a21c64f2-1b4b-54b3-a8a1-70ca63330e04" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049923}] :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069036} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069139} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069163} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487289} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487296} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487318} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487406} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492883} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545817} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545838} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186659664} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186659679} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186659752}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving Equations With Radical Solutions"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186070019 :tutorial/content "When an equation contains an $x^2$ term and a constant, it can be solved using the **square root method**. The square root method makes use of the fact that\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{x^2} = \\vert x \\vert \\,.\n$$\n\nTo illustrate, we can solve the equation $2x^2-18=0$ using the following three steps:\n\n1\\. Isolate the $x^2$ on one side of the equation:\n\n  $$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}-18 & =0 \\\\ 2{x}^{2} & =18 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n2\\. Take the square root of both sides of the equation\n\n  $$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{9} \\\\ |x| & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n3\\. Solve the resulting absolute value equation:\n\n  $$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =3\\,\\text{or}\\,x=-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, there are two solutions, $x=3$ and $x=-3.$ We can write this more concisely as $x= \\pm 3,$ where the \"$\\pm$\" symbol stands for \"plus or minus.\"" :tutorial/id #uuid "7ef92091-e041-5715-ac4e-75fa146c4cea" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8692 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096617 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096417} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b194bf2f-feaa-5656-a3ee-0ff466a39e77" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048593} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050420}] :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186657608} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097154} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097244} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233774} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233808} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234296} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240845} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287942} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299745} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299746} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299752} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530581} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530582} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530585} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530605} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530609} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530616} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530624} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530627} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530628} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530641} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186571442} {