17592186233776} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233814} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235431} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235479} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299797} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299798} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299799} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329890} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329891} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329892} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329947} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514464} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536061} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536065} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536069} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536071} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536074} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536075} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536077} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536087} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536090} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536098} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186571432} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186578806} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186600798} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186610506} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186610512} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186610517} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186610519} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186610528} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186615560}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Slope of the Tangent Line to a Product  at a Given Point"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096655 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096454} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f4b340f6-0ac6-5bbf-a7d4-0088ca3c4e5e" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048332} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048754} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050628}] :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186702107} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097099} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097217} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250122} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269142} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269147} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269150} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297312} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319344} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319345} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319346} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323783} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332860} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486565} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486579} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486582} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186581656} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186581662} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186581722} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186581747} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186637259} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186637270} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186656242} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186656251} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186701896} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186701903} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186701908} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186702076} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186702082} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186702087} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186702097} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186702101} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705331} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705332} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705335} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705340}] :knowledge-point/title "Integrating the Exponential Function With a Linear Exponent"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096656 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096455} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "19fd901f-e08d-54c5-9cda-68543a3d71a9" :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705465} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097220} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097221} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297314} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297389} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297390} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297396} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319347} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319348} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319349} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319350} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319351} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493015} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493018} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186581694} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186581720} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186581732} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186637237} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186701883} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186701887} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186701889} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186702429} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186702431} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186702437} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186702438} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186702446} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705454} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705457} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705458} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705461}] :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a General Exponential Function With a Linear Exponent"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097818 :tutorial/content "Suppose that we have the two functions $u(x)=2x^3$ and $v(x)=x^2.$ How do we calculate the derivative of their product $(u(x) \\cdot v(x))'?$\n\nTo do that, we can use the **product rule**, which states that the derivative of the product of two functions is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\left(u(x) \\cdot  v(x)\\right)' = u'(x) \\cdot v(x) + u(x) \\cdot v'(x).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the derivatives of the two functions are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(x) & =(2{x}^{3})^{′}=6{x}^{2}, \\\\ {v}^{′}(x) & =({x}^{2})^{′}=2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we plug all of this into the product rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(u(x)\\cdot v(x))}^{′} & =(6{x}^{2})({x}^{2})+(2{x}^{3})(2x) \\\\  & =6{x}^{4}+4{x}^{4} \\\\  & =10{x}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis example was quite simple, and we could have solved it by calculating the product first and then taking the derivative. We can do it now to double check our result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(u(x)\\cdot v(x))}^{′} & =(2{x}^{3}\\cdot {x}^{2})^{′} \\\\  & =(2{x}^{5})^{′} \\\\  & =10{x}^{4}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSometimes, however, we cannot calculate the product algebraically, or it can be tedious to do it. In those cases, the product rule will turn out to be very useful!" :tutorial/id #uuid "958f67f2-8e00-5818-8af7-9f88561ef84c" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2622 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097819 :tutorial/content "The product rule can also be written using *Leibniz notation*:\n\nIf $y = u(x) \\cdot v(x),$ then \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x} \\cdot  v + u \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}v}{\\textrm{d}x}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "3c6d5393-f91c-57e5-999c-92eedf550f2e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2623 :tutorial/title "Leibniz Notation"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097917 :tutorial/content "We know how to integrate $e^x,$ but how do we integrate a function like $e^{2x}?$\n\nThe answer is to use a substitution. If we let $u=2x,$ then\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = 2\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\textrm d x = \\dfrac 1 2 \\textrm d u.\n$$\n\nNow, we can write our original integral in terms of the new variable $u,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {e}^{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int {e}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{u}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2x}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAfter solving an integral using substitution, we should always double-check that our result is correct. If we differentiate the result, then it should come out to the original integrand:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2x}+C) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{2x})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {e}^{2x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2x)+0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2x}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & ={e}^{2x}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "6118f768-6b97-5aab-b111-872192691536" :tutorial/math-academy-id 846 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097918 :tutorial/content "If you're comfortable with memorizing formulas, then it's worth noting that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {e}^{px+q}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{p}{e}^{px+q}+C, \\\\ \\int {a}^{px+q}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{p\\operatorname{ln}⁡a}{a}^{px+q}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf you don't like to memorize formulas, you can always use substitution to solve these types of integrals." :tutorial/id #uuid "8b28e9eb-6c3c-50ac-a081-2da5742f5f83" :tutorial/math-academy-id 849 :tutorial/title "General Formulas for Integrating Exponential Functions With Linear Exponents"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115376 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186114959} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8712f490-8fef-56d2-bb8e-4f214b1121ad" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048746} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050076} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050723}] :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115780} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115781} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115786} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363771} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363774} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363777} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363780} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363884}] :knowledge-point/title "Constructing and Solving a Falling Body Model With Negligible Drag"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115377 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186114958} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "787c0213-46a5-50ef-8592-60c9965170f0" :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115777} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115778} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115779} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363783} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363790} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363797} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391526} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396877}] :knowledge-point/title "Constructing a Falling Body Model With Linear Drag"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115378 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186114957} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "74b356ed-a543-598f-b7d6-ce04cffd3675" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049370}] :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115782} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115783} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115789} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363804} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363807} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363810} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363813}] :knowledge-point/title "Constructing and Solving a Falling Body Model With Linear Drag"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115379 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186114960} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "474ea374-b644-5444-9b04-f8d76187176f" :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115784} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115785} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363823} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363833} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363836} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363881}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Terminal Velocity"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116420 :tutorial/content "Now, consider the case when drag is *not* negligible. At low speeds, drag is often approximately proportional to the body’s velocity. This model is called **linear drag**.\n\nDefining the direction of motion (downwards) as positive, there are still two forces acting on the body:\n\n- The force due to gravity is the weight, which equals $mg$.\n- The drag force is $$D=-kv,$$ where $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality (this force is negative because it acts upward, opposite the motion).\n\nHence, the net force on the body is $$F = mg - kv.$$\n\nApplying Newton's second law, $F=ma$, where $a$ is the acceleration of the body, we obtain $$\\begin{aligned}F & =mg-kv \\\\ ma & =mg-kv \\\\ m\\,\\frac{dv}{dt} & =mg-kv\\end{aligned}$$\n\nFinally, writing in standard form, we get $$m\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}v}{\\mathrm{d}t} + kv = mg \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}v}{\\mathrm{d}t} + \\dfrac{k}{m}v = g}.$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "ba5da4c4-0ea9-5c2d-be40-eb2771cd469e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14512 :tutorial/title "Falling Body Problems With Linear Drag"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116421 :tutorial/content "**Terminal velocity** is the constant speed a falling object approaches when the upward resistive force (such as drag) becomes large enough to exactly balance the downward gravitational force.\n\nWhen drag is proportional to velocity, with a constant of proportionality $k>0,$ this balance occurs when $$mg - kv_\\text{term} = 0.$$\n\nSolving for the terminal velocity, we get $$v_\\text{term} = \\dfrac{mg}{k}.$$\n\nFor example, suppose a sealed electronics crate of mass $44\\,\\textrm{kg}$ falls vertically downward. Let $v$ be its velocity, with downward taken as the positive direction. The air resistance on the crate is proportional to its velocity with a constant of proportionality $k=8\\,\\textrm{kg/s}$ and acts opposite to the direction of motion.\n\nAssuming that the acceleration due to gravity is $g = 9.8\\,\\textrm{m/s}^2,$ and the only forces acting on the crate are the weight due to gravity and drag, what is the terminal velocity $v_\\text{term}$ of the crate?\n\nRecall that at terminal velocity (a constant), acceleration is zero, so by $F=ma,$ the resultant force is zero.\n\nFirst, note that only two forces act on the crate:\n\n- the force due to gravity, given by the weight $$w = mg = 44\\cdot9.8 = 431.2\\,\\textrm{N},$$\n- the force due to drag $D,$ which is proportional to the velocity (and negative since it acts in the negative direction), $$D=-kv = -8v.$$\n\nHence, the resultant force acting on the crate is $$F = w + D = 431.2 - 8v.$$\n\nWhen the crate reaches terminal velocity $v_\\text{term},$ the resultant force is zero: $$431.2 - 8v_\\text{term} = 0 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad v_\\text{term} = \\dfrac{431.2}{8} = 53.9$$\n\nTherefore, the terminal velocity of the crate is $v_\\text{term} = 53.9\\,\\textrm{m/s}.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "fd8a1b18-5477-5623-a9e8-2ec97f0ba38c" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14517 :tutorial/title "Terminal Velocity"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116422 :tutorial/content "Consider a body of mass $m$ falling vertically, influenced only by\n\n- gravity $mg$, and\n- air resistance (drag) $D$.\n\nAssume that both $m$ and $g$ are constant, and choose the downward direction as the positive direction.\n\n![](images/9e/9ecacfcd964cb91f1cc35c4b9abe8e0969b85c02703eaf34e26612a9a624fdcc.png)\n\nThere are two forces acting on the body:\n\n- The force due to gravity is the weight, which equals $mg$.\n- The drag force is $D \\leq 0$, since it acts upward (in the negative direction).\n\nHence, the net force on the body is $F = mg + D.$\n\nIf drag is negligible, then $D=0$, so the net force becomes $F=mg.$\n\nApplying Newton's second law, $F=ma$, where $a$ is the acceleration of the body, we get $$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle F & \\displaystyle  = m g \\\\ \\displaystyle m a & \\displaystyle  = m g \\\\ \\displaystyle a & \\displaystyle  = g .\\end{array}\n$$\n\nFinally, since acceleration is the derivative of velocity, $a=\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}v}{\\mathrm{d}t}$, we obtain $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}v}{\\mathrm{d}t} = g.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "f11421af-f9cf-52e8-a33a-db3d617d510d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14520 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116423 :tutorial/content "Suppose a supply drone of mass $3\\,\\textrm{kg}$ descends vertically. Let $v(t)$ be the drone's velocity, in meters per second, $t\\geq 0$ seconds after the descent begins, with downward taken as the positive direction.\n\nAssuming air resistance is negligible, and the only force acting on the drone is its weight due to gravity $g,$ let's find the differential equation that models the velocity.\n\nRecall Newton's second law, which states that the resultant force $F$ acting on a body equals the product of its mass $m$ and acceleration $a{:}$ $$F = ma$$\n\nSince air resistance is negligible, the only force acting on the drone is its weight due to gravity. The weight $w$ of the drone is given by $$\\begin{aligned}F & =w \\\\ & =mg \\\\ & =3g.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nNow, recall that acceleration is the derivative of velocity: $a=\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}v}{\\mathrm{d}t}.$ Substituting this into Newton's second law, along with our expression for the resultant force on the drone, we get\n\n$$3g = 3\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}v}{\\mathrm{d}t}$$\n\nFinally, simplifying and writing in standard form, we get\n\n$$\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}v}{\\mathrm{d}t} = g.$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "80f1fef2-b0b7-5402-a46e-aaf5c0c62471" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14525 :tutorial/title "A Worked Example"}]}
